claude_session_archive__projects__-workspace-SuperGrok1-5__122b3aee-fa74-43c0-936d-39ff1eca854f.jsonl.txt:19134:@@REC4553|attachment||2026-06-09T23:54:43.032Z@@ [ATTACHMENT] {"type": "queued_command", "prompt": "<task-notification>\n<task-id>ada2f0e60b2e71d6e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01A2iC6T2A8K2wXGELijpREN</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-0/-workspace-SuperGrok1-5/122b3aee-fa74-43c0-936d-39ff1eca854f/tasks/ada2f0e60b2e71d6e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"A4: race harness line-by-line audit\" completed</summary>\n<result>All confirmed:\n- `fast_val_eval` is set ONLY by the tuner (tune_optimizers.py:207), never in the race. So in the race the fast path is dead; `_eval_log` always does the full triple eval.\n- `_fin` (684-697) **never reads `st.best_test_acc`** \u2192 it's dead (set but never consumed) AND under `early_stop_on=\"val\"` it actually holds val acc (misnomer).\n- `EarlyStopper.step(self, test_acc, ...)` \u2014 the parameter is named `test_acc` but receives `stop_acc` which is `va` under val-stopping \u2192 the name lies (confirmed).\n- The race default `early_stop_on` is unset \u2192 defaults to `\"test\"` \u2192 **the shipped race stops on TEST acc**. Only the tuner sets `\"val\"`. The advisor's correction #1 is right; I'll write it that way.\n\nI have fully verified every load-bearing claim. Now writing the final audit report.\n\n---\n\n# READ-ONLY AUDIT \u2014 `grokking_race_v2.py` (region A4, 2046 lines)\n\nScope: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_race_v2.py`. No edits made. Cross-checked against `grokking_optimizers/dispatch.py`, the optimizer side-methods, and `tuning/tune_optimizers.py`. Line numbers are the current file state (the file is being actively modified; all citations verified against the live snapshot).\n\n## Premise correction (important)\nThe task brief states \"the race records `grokking_step` on val\" and asks to spec a test-confirmation field. **The shipped race actually stops on TEST accuracy.** `_stopper` (line 612-617) keys on `c.get(\"early_stop_on\",\"test\")`; nothing in `DEFAULT_CONFIG`, the `_common` kwargs (1944-1958), or argparse ever sets `early_stop_on=\"val\"`. Only the **tuner** sets `\"val\"` (tune_optimizers.py:197) + `patience=1`. So the fake-grok exposure is a *tuner* property, and any future \"val-criterion race\" \u2014 not the shipped race. Findings below are framed on the verified code, not the brief's premise.\n\n---\n\n## SEVERITY: HIGH\n\n### H1 \u2014 Patience is unreachable in the shipped race \u2192 every run runs to `max_steps`; wall-time maximally inflated\n`DEFAULT_CONFIG[\"eval_every\"]=10` (line 242) is **overwritten** at `__main__` by `DEFAULT_CONFIG[\"eval_every\"]=args.eval_every` (line 1980), whose argparse default is **100** (line 1865). `_common` also passes `eval_every=args.eval_every` (line 2049). So under `python grokking_race_v2.py` the effective `eval_every=100`, and the literal `10` + its \"owner: every 10 gradient steps\" comment are dead.\nConsequence: `max_steps`/`early_stop_max_steps` default to 20 000 (line 1979/1862) \u21d2 at most ~200 evals per run, but `early_stop_patience=500` (line 252) is counted **in evals** (EarlyStopper `_counter` increments once per `step()` call, line 514). 200 &lt; 500 \u21d2 `_counter` can never reach `patience` \u21d2 the `*_threshold` stop at line 515-518 **never fires**. Every run terminates via the `current_step &gt;= max_steps` branch (line 504-507) with `stopping_reason=\"max_steps\"`, burning the full 20 000 steps regardless of how early it groks. `grokking_wall`/`grokking_step` are still recorded at first threshold crossing (line 511-513), so \"time to grok\" is correct, but `wall_time`/`total_steps` (and the bar-chart's DNF fallback `r.grokking_wall or r.wall_time`) are all pinned to the max-steps ceiling.\nSemantic-shift note the brief asked for: patience is in EVALS, not steps. Effective post-grok hold = `patience \u00d7 eval_every` steps. At the dead default eval_every=10 it would be 5 000 steps; at the live eval_every=100 it is 50 000 steps \u2014 i.e. *larger than max_steps*, which is why it never triggers. Either lower `e
claude_session_archive__projects__-workspace-SuperGrok1-5__122b3aee-fa74-43c0-936d-39ff1eca854f.jsonl.txt:19146:@@REC4565|attachment||2026-06-09T23:55:21.225Z@@ [ATTACHMENT] {"type": "queued_command", "prompt": "<task-notification>\n<task-id>adc93b5cb433094d4</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01Rfg37tJANijv9jnpJFCLRr</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-0/-workspace-SuperGrok1-5/122b3aee-fa74-43c0-936d-39ff1eca854f/tasks/adc93b5cb433094d4.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"A2: bindings.cpp line-by-line audit\" completed</summary>\n<result>This confirms the complete picture of validation coverage. Every vector binding validates `params` vs `grads` length (or grads vs ema_bufs for grokfast EMA), but NONE validates the secondary lists (exp_avgs, exp_avg_sqs, emas, mus, sharpness_cache, slows, steps, layer_alphas, layer_beta1s, s_bufs, param_inits, gru_states). And `supergrok2_prepare_and_batched_step` (2189) has NO validation call at all \u2014 confirmed by its absence from this grep.\n\nI have all the evidence. Let me write the final audit report. Per instructions, I'll return it directly as my final message (not a file).\n\nLet me compose the report now.\n\n# READ-ONLY Audit \u2014 Region A2: C++ Binding Layer\n\n**Scope:** `csrc/bindings/bindings.cpp` (3491 lines), `csrc/bindings/helpers.h` (238 lines), `csrc/bindings/dispatch.cpp` (688 lines, **PROVISIONAL** \u2014 sibling agent refactoring; fused_step body tagged provisional, not deep-dived). Cross-referenced all 11 optimizer call sites in `grokking_optimizers/optimizers/*.py` and the linked `_ops.cpython-311-x86_64-linux-gnu.so`.\n\n## Headline result (the thing the brief was hunting)\n\n**The recently-added `slows` insertion is CORRECT \u2014 no off-by-one.** Verified arg-by-arg against both live call sites:\n- `supergrok2_batched_step` (C++ 1899-1932): Python passes `\u2026, active_mus, active_slows, active_gru_states, \u2026` (supergrok2.py:2280-2282) \u2192 matches C++ `mus, slows, gru_states` (1905-1907).\n- `supergrok2_prepare_and_batched_step` (C++ 2189-2224): Python passes `\u2026, mus, slows, sharpnesses, \u2026` (supergrok2.py:1674-1677) \u2192 matches C++ `mus, slows, sharpnesses` (2195-2197).\n- Subtle correctness confirmed: the two SG2 entries order the GRU weights/biases *differently* (`batched`: `gru_Wz,gru_bz,gru_Wr,gru_br,gru_Wh,gru_bh` interleaved; `prepare`: `gru_Wz,gru_Wr,gru_Wh,gru_bz,gru_br,gru_bh` grouped) and **each matches its own caller**.\n\n**All 11 optimizers cross-checked, all positional args agree** (adamw, grokadamw, grokfast, lion, looksam, muon, neuralgrok, prodigy, supergrok11, supergrok15, supergrok2). `prodigy_fused_step`'s widened return `(d_lr, r_ema, s_ema)` is correctly unpacked at prodigy.py:227. SG11/SG15 23-arg calls match. MoE 5 call sites match.\n\n**Why dimension 6 is otherwise closed by construction:** the `.so` links and exposes `PyInit__ops`, which *proves* C++ binding\u2194launcher signature agreement (any drift would change name-mangling and fail to link/load). The only unguarded ABI surface is the Python\u2192pybind *positional* mapping, because every `m.def` except `fused_step` carries **no `py::arg` names** and the multi-tensor args are all `torch::Tensor` (so pybind type-dispatch cannot catch a reorder). That positional surface is exactly what was hand-verified above.\n\n## ISSUES\n\n### HIGH\n\n**H1 \u2014 `supergrok2_prepare_and_batched_step` performs ZERO boundary validation** \u2014 `bindings.cpp:2189-2273`\nIt calls neither `check_params_grads` nor `check_param_grad` (confirmed: absent from the validation grep), yet indexes `grads[i]`, `steps[i]`, `layer_alphas[i]`, `layer_beta1s[i]`, `sharpnesses[i]` (2236-2248) and forwards `params/grads/\u2026` to a launcher that treats `data_ptr()` as a dense `[numel]` buffer. Its sibling `supergrok2_batched_step` *does* call `check_params_grads` (2235). The load-bearing gap is **contiguity**: `_flat_params.append(p)` stores the raw `nn.Parameter` with no `.contiguous()` (supergrok2.py:1360), and `.data` is passed (supergrok2.py:1669). helpers.h:130-135 explicitly documents that a non-contiguous param silently corrup
